{"full":"hf:shreyask/system-one-two","spaceId":"shreyask/system-one-two","name":"System 1 + 2","source":"huggingface","description":"A small decision model decides when Gemma 4 is needed","category":"AI & machine learning","language":"HTML","topics":["static","region:us"],"technologies":[],"likes":3,"created":"2026-10-07T02:35:49.000Z","updated":"2026-10-07T02:51:41.000Z","license":null,"runtime":"RUNNING","appUrl":"https://shreyask-system-one-two.static.hf.space/","demo":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/shreyask/system-one-two","availability":"available","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T20:59:39.241Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-10T20:59:39.241Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-10T20:59:39.241Z","color":"#3c3250","ink":"#e4d4ff","symbol":"🧲","overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Most chat messages don't need a large language model. Here, every message is first matched against your memories (notes, photos and voice memos stuck to a fridge) with EmbeddingGemma 2. Then Strands Decider 2B, a System One decision model run with open-jev, decides whether one of those memories already answers it. If one does, the answer is the memory itself, in a few hundred milliseconds. If not, Gemma 4 E2B writes the answer, with the closest memories as context. A meter counts how often Gemma was actually needed."],"features":["Drop photos or voice memos on the fridge: Gemma 4 describes each one once, then it's searchable.","\"How it decided\" shows exactly what System One read: memories with match strength in words, never scores.","\"Still ask Gemma\" puts the memory answer and Gemma's answer side by side.","Everything runs in your browser on WebGPU (about 4.5 GB of models on first load, cached after). On phones the memory route runs with the 345 MB GLiNER2.5-Decide mobile build; Gemma 4 needs a desktop."],"sourceUrl":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/shreyask/system-one-two/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"9e409c1aa15ccc9bd77fb982c6b06d7ce4c160866af51a693b2e0d9b4e7ffe64","extractedAt